62 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H5
/P 48 0 R
/T (on which core and when. A good scheduling decision keeps every core busy and lets a thread keep using the data already stored in the cache of the core it ran on. A poor decision can leave some cores idle while others are overloaded, or can move a thread to a new core where its cached data is lost. [4])
/E (on which core and when. A good scheduling decision keeps every core busy and lets a thread keep using the data already stored in the cache of the core it ran on. A poor decision can leave some cores idle while others are overloaded, or can move a thread to a new core where its cached data is lost. [4])
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
63 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H5
/P 48 0 R
/T (Two factors strongly influence this behaviour. The first is processor affinity, which controls the set of cores on which a thread is allowed to run \(for example, using taskset or os.sched_setaffinity\(\) on Linux\). The second is workload distribution, which is the way work is divided among the threads or processes \(for example, fixed equal blocks of work versus work taken dynamically from a shared queue\).)
/E (Two factors strongly influence this behaviour. The first is processor affinity, which controls the set of cores on which a thread is allowed to run \(for example, using taskset or os.sched_setaffinity\(\) on Linux\). The second is workload distribution, which is the way work is divided among the threads or processes \(for example, fixed equal blocks of work versus work taken dynamically from a shared queue\).)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
64 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H5
/P 48 0 R
/T (Pinning a thread to a core can keep its cache warm and avoid migrations, but it also prevents the scheduler from moving the thread to an idle core. Static distribution has very low overhead, but it can leave some workers with more work than others when tasks have unequal cost. Dynamic distribution balances the load, but adds coordination overhead. [1], [2], [3])
/E (Pinning a thread to a core can keep its cache warm and avoid migrations, but it also prevents the scheduler from moving the thread to an idle core. Static distribution has very low overhead, but it can leave some workers with more work than others when tasks have unequal cost. Dynamic distribution balances the load, but adds coordination overhead. [1], [2], [3])
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
65 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H5
/P 48 0 R
/T (In this research, I will study how processor affinity and workload distribution affect the execution performance of a CPU-bound parallel workload on Linux, using Python \(with the multiprocessing module and os.sched_setaffinity\) and an optional Rust extension for the computation kernel.)
/E (In this research, I will study how processor affinity and workload distribution affect the execution performance of a CPU-bound parallel workload on Linux, using Python \(with the multiprocessing module and os.sched_setaffinity\) and an optional Rust extension for the computation kernel.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [9]
>>
