324 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 54 0 R
/T (First, I will compare the affinity modes. If pinning gives almost the same time as the default scheduler, the Linux scheduler is already placing the workers well for this workload. If single is much slower, it confirms that restricting the allowed cores removes parallelism.)
/E (First, I will compare the affinity modes. If pinning gives almost the same time as the default scheduler, the Linux scheduler is already placing the workers well for this workload. If single is much slower, it confirms that restricting the allowed cores removes parallelism.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [23]
>>
endobj
325 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 54 0 R
/T (Next, I will compare the distribution methods. If block distribution has a high load imbalance and a longer execution time, it suggests that a few workers finished late while others sat idle. If dynamic distribution has an imbalance close to 1.0, the shared queue is balancing the load, although its queue overhead should also be checked.)
/E (Next, I will compare the distribution methods. If block distribution has a high load imbalance and a longer execution time, it suggests that a few workers finished late while others sat idle. If dynamic distribution has an imbalance close to 1.0, the shared queue is balancing the load, although its queue overhead should also be checked.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [24]
>>
endobj
326 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 54 0 R
/T (I will also check how speedup grows with the number of workers. If speedup stops growing at the number of available cores, extra workers are only competing for the same cores. These explanations will be checked against the actual results. The experiment will not assume that pinning, or more workers, always improves performance.)
/E (I will also check how speedup grows with the number of workers. If speedup stops growing at the number of available cores, extra workers are only competing for the same cores. These explanations will be checked against the actual results. The experiment will not assume that pinning, or more workers, always improves performance.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [25]
>>
endobj
327 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 54 0 R
/T (This research investigates how processor affinity and workload distribution affect execution performance on a multi-core Linux system. The prototype uses multiple worker processes, optional core pinning, and three methods of distributing tasks. Execution time, throughput, and load imbalance are measured for different configurations.)
/E (This research investigates how processor affinity and workload distribution affect execution performance on a multi-core Linux system. The prototype uses multiple worker processes, optional core pinning, and three methods of distributing tasks. Execution time, throughput, and load imbalance are measured for different configurations.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [26]
>>
endobj
328 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 54 0 R
/T (The experiment is intended to identify which combination of affinity and distribution gives better performance for the selected workload. The final conclusion will be based on the measured values and graphs, and will explain whether the results support the hypotheses.)
/E (The experiment is intended to identify which combination of affinity and distribution gives better performance for the selected workload. The final conclusion will be based on the measured values and graphs, and will explain whether the results support the hypotheses.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [27]
>>
endobj
329 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 54 0 R
/T (The experiment has several limitations. It uses one CPU-bound workload, one machine, and one set of task costs, so the results may not apply to every system or workload. The measurements are affected by the processor model, SMT, the Linux kernel version, power settings, and other background activity. The prototype also does not directly measure cache misses or CPU migrations.)
/E (The experiment has several limitations. It uses one CPU-bound workload, one machine, and one set of task costs, so the results may not apply to every system or workload. The measurements are affected by the processor model, SMT, the Linux kernel version, power settings, and other background activity. The prototype also does not directly measure cache misses or CPU migrations.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [28]
>>
endobj
330 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 54 0 R
/T (In future work, the experiment could use perf to count migrations and cache misses, test memory-bound and mixed workloads, compare thread pinning on NUMA machines, implement a full work-stealing scheduler in Rust, and compare different Linux scheduling policies such as SCHED_FIFO or nice priorities.)
/E (In future work, the experiment could use perf to count migrations and cache misses, test memory-bound and mixed workloads, compare thread pinning on NUMA machines, implement a full work-stealing scheduler in Rust, and compare different Linux scheduling policies such as SCHED_FIFO or nice priorities.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [29]
>>
endobj
