/Title (Two factors strongly influence this behaviour. The first is processor affinity, which controls the set of cores on which a thread is allowed to run \(for example, using taskset or os.sched_setaffinity\(\) on Linux\). The second is workload distribution, which is the way work is divided among the threads or processes \(for example, fixed equal blocks of work versus work taken dynamically from a shared queue\).)
/Dest [10 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 353 0 R
/Prev 456 0 R
/Next 518 0 R
>>
endobj
518 0 obj
<<
/Title (Pinning a thread to a core can keep its cache warm and avoid migrations, but it also prevents the scheduler from moving the thread to an idle core. Static distribution has very low overhead, but it can leave some workers with more work than others when tasks have unequal cost. Dynamic distribution balances the load, but adds coordination overhead. [1], [2], [3])
/Dest [10 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 353 0 R
/Prev 517 0 R
/Next 457 0 R
>>
endobj
